   "type": "markdown",
   "source": "# Analysis of Graphitic Carbon\n\n## Overview\nX-ray Photoelectron Spectroscopy (XPS) is a powerful surface-sensitive analytical technique used to characterize the chemical composition and bonding states of materials. This analysis focuses on graphitic carbon using the **C 1s core level** and **C KLL Auger** transitions.\n\n## C 1s Photoelectron Peak\nThe C 1s peak provides detailed information about carbon bonding environments in graphitic samples:\n\n- **C=C (sp\u00b2 hybridization)**: The dominant peak in graphitic carbon, typically at ~284.5 eV, representing aromatic C-C bonds in the graphene lattice\n- **\u03c0-\u03c0* shake-up satellite**: A characteristic feature of graphitic carbon at higher binding energy (~290-291 eV), arising from \u03c0\u2192\u03c0* transitions during photoemission\n- **C-C (sp\u00b3 hybridization)**: Defect sites or amorphous carbon at lower binding energy (~283 eV)\n- **C-O, C=O, O-C=O**: Oxidized carbon species from surface contamination or functional groups at higher binding energies (285-290 eV)\n\n## C KLL Auger Transition\nThe C KLL Auger line is produced when a core-level hole is filled by an outer-shell electron, with energy release causing ejection of another electron. This transition is particularly useful for:\n- **Oxidation state determination**: The kinetic energy and lineshape of C KLL Auger electrons are sensitive to the chemical environment\n- **Complementary analysis**: Provides independent confirmation of carbon bonding states identified in C 1s XPS\n- **Surface chemistry**: Distinguishes between different carbon functionalities without interference from the primary photoelectron signal\n\n## Interpretation Strategy\nBy analyzing both C 1s and C KLL data, we can obtain a comprehensive picture of graphitic carbon quality, degree of oxidation, and surface defect density.",
   "height": 735
  },
